  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current problem involves finding a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral, defined as the maximum correlation between h and (1 - h) over all shifts. The previous approach uses a genetic algorithm with gradient descent, but it may get stuck in local minima. \n\nA more structured optimization approach using convex programming can offer better performance by utilizing efficient solvers for constrained optimization. Even though the problem is non-convex due to h_i h_j terms, the problem can be represented in a form suitable for convex optimization solvers.\n\nWe propose to model the problem using CVXPY, a convex optimization package, leveraging the disciplined convex programming (DCP) ruleset. This allows us to express the problem with constraints for each possible shift and utilize efficient solvers like SCS or ECOS.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet m = n_points. We aim to find h_i \u2208 [0, 1] for i = 0, ..., m-1 such that:\n- sum_{i=0}^{m-1} h_i = m/2\n- For each shift k (integer, k \u2260 0), sum_{i=0}^{m-1 - |k|} h_i (1 - h_{i+|k|}) \u2264 C (scaled appropriately)\n\nThe problem is non-convex due to the h_i h_j terms in the overlap expressions. However, by formulating it as a constrained optimization problem within CVXPY, we can exploit its built-in capabilities to handle non-linear constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize with initial_h_values if available, ensuring feasibility by normalization.\nb. Define the CVXPY problem:\n   - Variables: h (size m) \u2208 [0,1], C \u2208 \u211d\n   - Constraints:\n     1. sum(h) = m/2\n     2. For each k from 1 to m-1 (shifts in positive and negative directions), compute the overlap and enforce \u2264 C.\nc. Use CVXPY to solve the problem with a suitable solver, starting from the initial guess.\nd. Project the solution back to feasible h values, ensuring sum(h) = m/2 and h_i \u2208 [0,1].\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Use initial_h_values as the initial guess to guide the solver towards better solutions.\n- Set solver parameters (max iterations, tolerance) to balance speed and accuracy.\n- Experiment with different solvers (e.g., SCS, ECOS) to find the most effective one.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimate that solving the problem with CVXPY will take approximately 600 seconds. Implement checks to stop solvers if they exceed the time budget, and return the best feasible solution found.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nAfter solving, validate the constraints:\n- Ensure h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n- Verify sum(h) = m/2\n- Recompute the maximum overlap to confirm the objective value.\n\nReturn the h values, the computed C, and the number of points m.",
